Newsgroups: alt.fan.robert.mcelwaine,alt.comp.acad-freedom.talk
From: kadie@eff.org (Carl M. Kadie)
Subject: Re: Internet is a resource, not a right
Message-ID: <1993May10.155758.29894@eff.org>
Date: Mon, 10 May 1993 15:57:58 GMT

Preface: I doubt that the university acted under the rationals to
which I'm responding. In other words, I'm arguing against these
rationals and not necessarily with the university's action.

mahalkdp@cnsvax.uwec.edu writes:

>The university set up rules governing the use of their system.

At most universities the rules (in the student code) are no
"institutional censorship" and "discussion and expression of all views
is permitted within the University subject only to requirements for
the maintenance of order." [To take two rules from the U. of Illinois
student code]

State universities must also follow another set of rules, the
constitution. As interpreted by the courts, it forbids (among other
things) viewpoint-base discrimation in created publically-owned
forums. It also requires due process before a government agent can
take away an entitlement.

>They own the Internet nameservers and equipment, and so they have the
>final say in how their eqipment is used. At UW-Eau Claire, the usage
>should be related to and should not conflict with the University's
>stated goals.

Among the goals at most universities are "the transmission of
knowledge and the pursuit of truth". Free expression and intellectual
freedom on campus are generally considered to be important for
achieving these goals. (See for example, the U. of Wisconsin
at Milwaukee netnews policy)


>I know because network gaming (MUDs) got out of hand last November,
>people complained, and we are now banned from using University
>hardware for ANY games Sun-Fri 6am-11pm.  ...according to this
>McElwaine disagreement, these game players have a RIGHT to use the
>network, and so they should be able to continue without the
>University having any say in what the game players use the equipment
>for.

Free expression is special. It is different than game playing. It is
explicitly protected by the constitution and probably by the
university code.

>Note that the network here is treated as a resource for students and faculty.
>If this resource is abused (like with the gaming), it can be taken away.

But if the resource is a public forum, the school should obey the
rules about public forums. Also, if the resource is removed
as punishment, the school should obey the rules about due process.

>Not all students have network access... they must fill out a form requesting
>an account first on the VAX, and then they can use the Internet or system.
>Because of this, I feel that the students do not have a 'right' to use the
>system and have an account.. they must ask to be allowed to use the system and
>resources first. This, IMHO, makes the University the supreme authority on
>system use, since they are allowing access.
[...]

From what you describe, network access at your school is an
entitlement and so removal-as-punishment requires due process.

- Carl


