                      The Cutting Edge of Multiculturalism
                                by David Sacks
                                    Junior
                             Stanford University


     "I couldn't have taught this class ten years ago," declares Stanford
Professor Kennell Jackson to an overflowing classroom on the first day of
Spring quarter. "But people don't look at me like I'm crazy anymore -- what
history does has broadened considerably." And Jackson is not exaggerating.
"Black Hair as Culture and History," his ambitious new upper-level history
seminar, addresses how black hair" has interacted with the black presence in
this country -- how it has played a role in the evolution of black society."

     Turning from the chalkboard, Jackson asks whether anyone has experienced
"A Black Hair Event" recently. One student pipes up from the back of the
room: "Juliette Lewis' cornrow hair at the Oscars." "That's a good one,"
Jackson agrees, producing a picture of the actress from USA Today. "Any
others? No? Well, look for next week."

     If not for Jackson's earnestness, one might mistake the class for a
parody of multiculturalism. Scheduled lectures include "The Rise of the Afro"
and "Fade-O-Rama, Braiding and Dreadlocks," and local hair stylists will
visit class for a week of discussions. Enrolled students will view the 1960s
musical Hair and read Willie L. Morrow's 400 Years Without a Comb, Dylan
Jones' Haircults, and Michael Jackson's hit pop single "Man in the Mirror."

     As the multiculturalists succeed in overhauling University life, classes
like "Black Hair" are becoming the norm at Stanford. From speech codes to
condom giveaways to a grape juice boycott (in solidarity with the farmers'
union) to a "1960s" theme dorm, "multiculturalism" is not marked by any
coherent set of educational objectives but rather serves as a catch-all
phrase for the agenda of the American left. The rule of thumb is that if
something fits the political agenda of the campus lobby, then it is
"multicultural," and if it does not fit, then it is "politically incorrect."

     Jackson is right that much has changed at Stanford in the last ten
years. Led by President Donald Kennedy, who left Stanford this past summer,
the university has sought to institutionalize "new thinking and new
structures which incorporate diversity," and has granted the newly-formed
Office for Multicultural Development sweeping powers to ensure the campus'
"transformation." OMD Director Sharon Parker has acceded to the highest
policy making body at Stanford, the University Cabinet, where several dozen
fields in the School of Humanities and Sciences enjoy but a single voice
through their dean. The OMD offers financially starved departments monetary
bonuses for meeting hiring quotas and prods university programs "to select
students who have a commitment to a multicultural, diverse, and interactive
environment." Accordingly, despite a budget crisis, cash grants are always
available for courses like "Black Hair," which "enrich" knowledge of
"American racial minorities."

     With the now-notorious chant "Hey hey, ho ho, Western culture's got to
go," Stanford's multiculturalists insisted that the traditional curriculum be
made more "inclusive." Now we see what they had in mind. "Black Hair"
represents the logical conclusion of the same obsession with racial, gender,
and class differences that motivated "Cultures, Ideas, and Values," the
program that replaced the Western culture core program, as well as more
recent requirements in race relations and gender studies. Instead of helping
students to surmount superficial differences, the multiculturalists have
erected whole departments glorifying them. The new disciplines that promised
to "broaden horizons" and "open minds" in fact teach students that the afro
represents 1960s rebelliousness and that straightening one's hair is a symbol
of cultural subordination.

     Having removed all independent academic standards from the curriculum,
the multiculturalists have resorted to making everybody feel good. The
reasoning is that if minority students are more confident, then they will
perform better. Thus, "Black Hair" finds its niche as a self-help seminar:
African-American students may experience a sense of dignity at Jackson's
discovery that their ancestors were the preeminent wig makers of colonial
America. Hair dressers, too, may take pride in their inclusion on Stanford's
reading list. An academic climate once intended to foster individual thought
now promotes group therapy.

     As a practical matter, the deterioration of objective curricular
standards and the reluctance to oppose President Kennedy's multicultural
policies have left trendiness as the only guiding principle. No one wants to
seem less "tolerant" or "open-minded" than anyone else, as straying from the
politically correct line even a little bit might disappoint one's peers.
Nobody wants to lose this race to be the most politically correct, and so
even a course as trivial as "Black Hair" must be enthusiastically welcomed.
Building "multicultural communities" in reality produces a chorus of voices
all saying the same thing -- that Western culture has got to go.

     Without objective standards to act as an external check, the
multiculturalists have rejected any sense of accountability. It is hardly
surprising that this sort of institutional arrogance was featured prominently
during Stanford's overhead research scandal. Throughout the 1980s, the
Stanford administration redirected hundreds of millions of dollars in
government research funds to the university's multicultural experiments as
well as to yacht parties, travel vacations, the president's wedding
reception, and lavish flowers in his home. Kennedy's response typified the
attitudinal problem: "I wouldn't be embarrassed about saying that every damn
flower in the house ought to be an indirect cost against research."

     Were taxpayer funds actually improving the next generation of American  
minds, Stanford's arrogance could perhaps be pardoned. But students, as well
as taxpayers, are being hustled. Perspiration dripping from his forehead,
Jackson attempts to disguise his banal subject with high-browed lingo: "Black
hair has interacted with society, and today I'm trying to make it into a
field. You wouldn't find the same interaction in Africa. You don't find the
conflict over whose hair should be what, in what dimensions." Moreover, he
intones, "The term itself is homogeneous. It allows people to avoid what
black hair is. This is a very real issue, that there is this thing -- that we
are assuming is called -- 'black hair."'

     The bitter irony is that multiculturalism hurts the very students who
have been led to believe in it most. Eager and talented minority students
come to Stanford hoping to solve racial division, poverty, homelessness, and
other problems plaguing their communities. Instead, they will learn about
"The Great Debate." The Great Debate struggles with the dilemma over whether
to "straighten" one's hair or to remain "natural." One wonders how Martin
Luther King would have reacted to the implication that one of the most
important things a black student can think about is his hair.

     The multiculturalists say all that is needed to create a brave new
world, a diverse and egalitarian utopia, is more multiculturalism. Perhaps.
But at Stanford $20,000 per year seems like a steep price to pay for learning
which hair style is politically correct.

