Subject: WSU ACS Policy (_DRAFT_)
Message-ID: <1993May12.010919.922@eff.org>
Date: Wed, 12 May 1993 01:09:19 GMT

Summary: It is a great draft. It protects due process, free
expression, and private. It is being created with wide participation.
I only have a few minor comments.

Details:

>DRAFT | DRAFT!  This is a draft document.  I've not even put it through a
>DRAFT | speller. 
>DRAFT |
>DRAFT | 
>DRAFT |                           Appropriate Use of 
>DRAFT |                        Winona State University's 
>DRAFT |                      Academic Computing Facilites 
[...]
>DRAFT | The consequences for violation of acceptable use will usually be
>DRAFT | the suspension of of access and perhaps probation,
>DRAFT | suspension from Winona State University or in the case of a
>DRAFT | salaried employee, even termination is possible.  The exact
>DRAFT | consequence will vary depending upon the specific violation.  For
>DRAFT | example, seriously destructive computing could be considered
>DRAFT | destruction of state property and the person subject to civil
>DRAFT | action. 

I think it would be better to say that the consequences "may include"
rather than they "will usually include". Also, don't forget that a
very common consequence for a minor student violation is a formal
warning. (Indeed, at some schools the only punishments are informal
warning, formal warning, short suspension, expulsion.)

[...]
>DRAFT | * copying licensed software from lab micros or from the multiuser
>DRAFT | systems for personal use is considered theft. 
[...]

This is not strictly true. If the software is copyrighted (as almost
all is), it is considered copyright infringment not theft. If the
copying violates a license (not all software is licensed), then the
copying a license violation. I suggest the lines:

" * copying software in violation of copyright or license from the lab
micros or from the multiuser systems is illegal and prohibited."

- Carl
