Surely it depends whether the court rules that the University was
forbidding his access because of what he said, or about whether it was
because of his abuse of facilities.  From what I have heard of this
case, it sounds as if it was pulled for the latter, not the former.

If I posted a copy of /vmunix to the net once a day, in 20 random
newsgroups, and refused to stop, would you say that my University had the
right to pull my access?  Make it 40 newsgroups... or 100 -- then make it
a copy of one of Shakespeare's plays (the ones that are freely available).
When do I get stopped?

(This argument has already been put forward by some one else -- I have
still not seen a reply to it.)

Is that behaviour acceptable?  Should my net access be pulled?  What about
all those people who read the newsgroups and *pay* for their feeds
-- should they be made to pay more because some person decides that,
regardless of the newsgroup title, they will post their opinions and ideas
to it and continue to do so, repeatedly, even when requested to post to
more appropriate forums?

If McElwaine had kept his ideas to more appropriate newsgroups (i.e.,
appropriate to the subject he was talking about) he would have not been
in the situation he is now -- he had *plenty* of warning, and has no
one to blame but himself for not heeding it -- he can surely not claim
ignorance, anyway.

*I* would support his right to have net access re-instated if I believed
it had been pulled because of his views and what he said.  It's just
that I don't think that that was the case.

Phil Richards

