Dozens (hundreds?) of people wrote: (paraphrased)
>Suppressing McElwaine is CENSORSHIP!!!!  1st Amendment!  1st Amendment!

Let's look at this from a slightly different perspective.

Some time ago, this newsgroup had a rollicking discussion on the legal
status of Usenet.  After some haggling, most of the participants (inclu-
ding both Carl and me -- *gasp*) agreed that Usenet could fall under the
"limited public forum" doctrine.  Here's an excerpt from the "limited
public forum" doctrine:

	"Although a State is not required to indefinitely retain the open
	 character of the facility, as long as it does so it is bound by the
	 same standards as apply to a traditional public forum.  Reasonable
	 time, place and manner regulations are permissible, and a content-
	 based prohibition must be narrowly drawn to effectuate a compelling
	 state interest."

         [Interested parties can find this document on ftp.eff.org; look for]
         [Issue 1, Volume 25 of CAF-News.  It's also available via gopher.  ]

I do not believe that the prohibitions against McElwaine are content-based.
I, for one, have no problem with the content of his postings; most of his
detractors seem to oppose the repetitive, net-wide *method* of his postings.

{ enter speculative mode }
As I understand it, he has been in this particular fire for some time.  The
UW-EC folks have apparently told him (several times) that, while the content
of his materials is not objectionable, their widespread distribution (and the
failure to use crossposting, which would minimize the impact of his actions
on other sites) is improper.  After all, we're talking about Larsonian physics
in rec.sport.basketball.college!
{ end speculative mode }

This seems to be a 'time, place and manner' restriction, as opposed to a
content-based alternative.  He has repeatedly violated this restriction.
I don't believe that this is a censorship issue; if we accept the notion 
that Usenet is a limited public forum, we should also accept the notion 
of 'time, place and manner' restrictions.

If there are any participants in this discussion who believe that posting
identical materials to several dozen irrelevant newsgroups (on a regular 
basis) is not a reasonable subject for a 'time, place and manner' restric-
tion, I'd like to hear their rationale.

