As has been stated many times, it was not the content of McElwaine's
posts that got him booted off the net.  It was his blatant violation of
the rules governing the use of Usnet.  Not to mention violations of
netiquette, but that is not enforceable save by public scorn.

Free speech is guaranteed by the constitution, but, perhaps paridoxically,
there is no guarantee of access to any mode of expression.  You are not
given the *right* to air your views on TV, on the radio, in a newspaper;
the editors or equivalents can reject your work at will unless it is 
covered by specific laws (e.g. "fair hearing" laws for political issues).
Worse, if you haven't got the cash to get a TV spot, you don't get one.

Similarly for publication; the Astrophysical Journal is by no means
"required" to publish anything you send them; by far the opposite.  If
you want to publish your work independantly, you first have to find a
willing publisher.  And so forth.

THE CONSTITUTION GUARANTEES YOUR RIGHT TO SAY WHAT YOU WISH.  BUT IT DOES
NOT GUARANTEE YOU A MEDIUM IN WHICH TO SAY IT!!!!

Usnet (or Internet, or whatever) is such a medium.  Those who set it up,
like those who set up a publishing company, have the right under law
tho set up there own rules for what may or may not be posted, and how.
You may disagree with this - I do in many ways - but legally, McElwaine
has no legs to stand on if he broke his site's rules.

Finally, for those who still insist on claiming that McElwaine's posts
were banned because "people didn't like what he said", hop around the
newsgroups for a while.  Sci.astro or .physics will show you many
similar 'pseudoscientific' posts; the 'holocaust never happened' post
has been equalled or surpassed in outrageousness many times (the first
group to come to mind is alt.skinheads, but I may wrong them).  And
so forth.  All his views have been aired at some time or other by
someone else.  But those many 'someone elses' are not being booted off
the Net, because they followed the rules under which we here are 
permitted to air our views.  Net posting, *legally*, is a privelege
not a right.  If it were a right, we would have to guarantee *everyone* 
access.  Me, I think this would not be a bad idea.  But no one asked me.

--April (Airing views within the parameters set by UIUC)
