>not be up to the computer centre to censor what we can mail each 
>other, should it?
>
>Of course the University owns the computer network but even though Royal
>Mail owns its mailboxes and post offices it won't prohibit someone from
>mailing "obscene" material. 

Not so, under UK law. Section 11 of the Post Office Act 1953 prohibits
the enclosure in a postal packet of "any indecent or obscene print, 
painting, photograph, lithograph, engraving, cinematographic film,
book, and written communication, or any indecent or obscene article
whether similar to the above or not". The prohibition applies whether
or not the posting is solicited, and there is no public good defence
available.

In addition, S4 of the 1971 Unsolicited Goods and Services Act provides:
"A person shall be guilty of an offence if he sends or causes to be
sent to another person any book, magazine or leaflet (or advertising
material for any such publication) which he knows or ought
reasonably to know is unsolicited and which describes or 
illustrates human sexual techniques."

>
>And the line that goes "anyone found" must imply that they are willing
>to look into people's personal mail? 

Not necessarily - the authorities could simply act on complaints
from recipients.

Isn't the proper test whether or not the allegedly obscene
communication is solicited or likely to be welcome? I'd have thought 
that what the authorities are worried about is the circulation of
material to people who don't want it - which could be reasonably
construed as a form of harassment.

