In soc.culture.british, Paul Roberts (proberts@informix.com)
wondered about someone's comment about Canada
> |  Canada .. while also having a free speech clause does allow for
> |  bans on "hate speech" ..
> Have I got this wrong, or is it the case that in Canada it is actually
> a crime to deny that the Holocaust took place?

Canada has well-intentioned laws against promoting hatred
against identifiable groups, and such good intentions may be
paving a road to a politically-correct hell.  There is pressure
from some to have Holocaust-denial *interpreted* as promoting
hatred against Jews.  Of course, no doubt *some* people who deny
the Holocaust wish to promote hatred against Jews, and one can
sympathise with those who fear Jew-haters, but that is beside
the point to those of us concerned about logic, some/all
distinctions, civil liberties, due process, free speech, free
inquiry, etc, etc.  Should those who wish deny free speech
because they think it "hateful" succeed, I suspect the next step
will be to have, say, the mere polemical rejection of the
equality of women interpeted as sexual abuse, or  mere
asserting of the value of traditional disciplining of children
interpreted as child abuse.

To someone, such as myself, who was raised to respect what
"Hyde Park Corner" (OK, Marble Arch, to be more precise) stood
for, the current witch-hunt mentality in Canada is scary.

Suppose one was to wonder, for example, whether the current
climate of political correctness in Canada might be due, in
part, to the balance of immigration swinging away from cultures
to whom the values of "Hyde Park Corner" are vitally important
toward cultures to whom such values are very minor.  Such mere
speculation would draw accusations labelled with one of the
modern terms for witchcraft, e.g. "racism".  And "racism" in
Canada (like "sexism", "homophobia" etc. etc.) is only a hop,
skip and a jump away from being against the law.

