Subject: Re: The Case Against Bob
Message-ID: <1993May13.141826.3198@eff.org>
Date: Thu, 13 May 1993 14:18:26 GMT

Hypothetically, in Bob's defense:
(Feel free to add to it or to critique it).

>I. He impeded the computing activities of others

No evidence was giving that people's computing activities where
actually impeded.

>   A. He over used Usenet.

This is unconstitutionally vague.

>     1. He was by far the largest poster from this site.

So what? By definition someone had to be the largest poster from this
site. Now that Bob is gone will you punish the new largest poster from
this site?

>     2. He was the largest general poster in the world.

Bob was not the largest poster in the world. He was among the largest,
but again so what? Someone had to be. Unless substantial interference
is shown, this is immaterial.

>   B. His posts were off-topic.

The limits of the topics are not well defined.  It can be argued that
the posts were not off-topic. Finally, although off-topic posts are
common (by others) are common, this is the only time they have ever
been applied.

>   C. Other
>     1. He duplicated his notes dozens of times rather than
>        posting one copy of an article.
[...]

Others post duplicate notes (either to different newsgroups or to the
same newsgroup at different times). They don't get in trouble. Also,
although it would be easy to make an explictly rule against such
posts, no such rule was made. Finally, Bob felt that duplicates
were the best way to communicate his message, since there was
no explicit prohibition on this, it should not be punished.

===
The real reason Bob was punished was to stop the content-based
complaints of outsiders.

I. Some outsiders have written that Bob's article about the Holocaust
   made them some mad that they decided to try to get him kicked off
   the computer.
II. Many outsiders have written that Bob's articles are offensive.
III. The computer staff was so eager to stop the outside criticism
     that they announced to the world the results of Bob's punishment.
     Such an announcement is illegal under the Family Educational
     Rights and Privacy Act and puts the University's federal funding
     in jeopardy.

- Carl

