>a license, and can get it revoked for tons of reasons.

And I bet, as long as the local regulators followed due process, NONE
of the hams so ham-strung ;-) ever sued successfully.

Good analogy, actually: the airwaves, in Canada and the US, are
considered a common, though limited, resource: there's only so much
bandwidth available (just like on the InterNet, right?).  Thus: strict
regulation of private and commercial use of the resource.  NO ONE ha$
an AB$OLUTE RIGHT to broadca$t their opinions/music/whatever.  (Ju$t
HAD to put in a few CAP$, didn't I? ;-)

The InterNet *might* be considered the same way (possibly not as
"common" though: ether is free, but the use of the equipment which
transmits the bitstreams costs someone, somewhere, real money).
Historically, UseNet has been a cooperative community: we all obey
certain rather loose "rules", and we continue to enjoy the resource.

But, if even a small number of people abuse the resource badly enough,
it becomes useless to *everyone*.  At that point, someone has to have
the balls to force the abusers to cease and desist, for the protection
of the rest of the community.

Maybe all we really need is slightly smarter software that imposes a
"posting quota".  At a guess, something like 100KB per week should
give most people as much chance to reasonably voice their opinions as
they need. (Or s/w that detects and prevents the bloated posting
pattern McE used, or... ideas, anyone? )

(As an aside: In Canada, you can lose your ham license for using
obscenities over the air.  A friend of mine who's into
UseNet-by-packet-radio told me that they were a little worried about
getting shut down by the CRTC: everyone knows how many four-letter
words pass through an average news-feed in a day.  Seems the CRTC
needs to catch up with the times... ;-)
