This is a critique of the U. of Wisconsin - Eau Claire computer
policy. Overall the policy is quite good with excellent participation
and privacy. Free expression is generally very good. My only main
suggestion involved being more specific about "information that in
some way endangers" and overuse of computer conferences. Due process
is also very good. The only suggest relates to the possibility that a
computer expulsion might be an extra-disciplinary sanction.


>                 UNIVERSITY OF WISCONSIN - EAU CLAIRE
>                   COMPUTING AND NETWORKING SERVICES
>                         COMPUTER AND NETWORK
>                           USAGE GUIDELINES

[...]
>Access to computing and networking resources is a privilege to which all
>University faculty, staff, and students are entitled.
[...]

It is wonderful that universities can offer university computer
access.

[...]
>In general, information stored on computers is considered confidential,
>whether protected by the computer system or not, unless the owner
>intentionally makes that information available to other groups or
>individuals. The University of Wisconsin - Eau Claire will assume that
>computer users wish the information they store on central and campus
>shared computing resources to remain confidential. UWEC computing
>centers will maintain the confidentiality of all information stored on
>their computing resources.

Great.
 
>IV. ACADEMIC FREEDOM

>Free expression of ideas is central to the academic process. University
>of Wisconsin - Eau Claire computer system administrators will not remove
>any information from individual accounts or from electronic bulletin
>boards maintained in individual accounts unless it is determined that: 

[...]
>	b. The information in some way endangers computing, networking
>	   resources, or the information of other users (e.g., a 
>	   computer worm, virus, or other destructive program).

I wonder if the machine code for COPS is considered "information that
in some way endangers ... the information of others." The source code?
An Netnews article that tells how to write a 10-line password breaker?

I think nonrunable text should definitely be allowed. I think source
code should probably be allowed. And maybe even machine code (although
perhaps with a requirement that it not be executable in one-step).  In
general "information" should be allowed; just as information on
lockpicking and bomb making is allowed in the library. The policy
could be improved by only banning programs (at some specific level of
compilation) and not the too general "information".

[...]
>unacceptable use of computing and networking resources include: 

[...]
>	f. Use of computer and/or network facilities in ways that 
>	   impede the computing activities of others (for example; 
>	   randomly initiating interactive electronic communications 
>	   or e-mail exchanges, overuse of interactive network 
>	   utilities, overuse of network accessible bulletin boards or 
>	   conferences, and the "off topic" posting of material
>	   bulletin boards and conferences).
[...]

What is over use? The policy could be improved with some specific
limit. Also, "off topic" seems very subjective and hard to enforce
fairly.

[...]
>Violation of the policies described herein for use of computing
>resources are dealt with seriously. Violators are subject to
>disciplinary procedures of the University and, in addition, may lose
>computing privileges.
[...]

This suggests that a computer explusion is not part of the
disciplinary procedure. I think the policy would be improved by
saying:

>Violators are subject to disciplinary procedures of the University
>which can result in, among other sanctions, a lose computing
>privileges.


>VII. APPEAL PROCESS

>To appeal a sanction, submit a letter to the Office of Student Affairs.
>The appeal will be reviewed by a Computing and Networking Services
>Review Panel. This panel is comprised of faculty and students. For
>additional information and assistance, contact the Office of Student
>Affairs.

Good!
