I don't know what the reasons were.  However, to the best of my knowledge,
the main problem with him is that he posts the same lengthy message to many
different newsgroups without crossposting.  When messages are crossposted,
typically only one message goes out, no matter how many newsgroups in the
list.  This is relatively efficient.  When messages are posted separately,
every single copy needs to be sent out.  This serves no useful purpose
other than supporting the laziness of the sender, who may produce shell
scripts that do the separate posting.  It wastes a considerable amount of
bandwidth.

This has been pointed out to him repeatedly, but he has chosen to continue
to act in this manner which is destructive to the net.

I do not think that there is any justification for revoking anybody's net
access on the basis of message content, no matter how loopy.  Personally, I
thought McElwaine was occasionally amusing.  I do think that there is
justification for revoking access due to a decision needlessly to destroy
some of the functionality of the net.

