>avoid him.

Oh, don't avoid him the first time around. Many of his posts are real
side-splitters. But you'll get a might peeved the tenth time you see
"Russia's OPERATIONAL Star Wars System" in one month.

>I tend to think people who post craziness are being humorous. It is
>a personal failing of mine, since I think most everything is funny.
>I forget that some people take themselves and their pet delusions
>VERY seriously. 

I have it on decent authority that McElwaine is of the latter kind: he
really BELIEVES all this crap he posts, and seems to feel he has an edict
from god to post it as often as possible.

>On a less nasty note, I always just skipped over his articles. If
>he was e-mailing them to people, that would be the best reason to
>yank priveleges. I assume, however, he was warned repeatedly to
>cease and desisst (posting diatribes) and that he did not. Is this
>the case? 

Yes. He was asked nicely repeatedly.

>If so, then he was antagonizing possibly tens of
>thousands of netters who pay to view each thread and who had to
>endure repeated postings of the same rants. Not fair or right to
>the majority of users. That is my opinion only.
>I have no personal animosity towards Mr. McElwaine. I am able to
>avoid reading his posts. Those who could not and had to pay for
>them probably have their own feelings about him, however.


Yep. Remember, not everybody gets Net News from a 20 GB VAX server over a
10Mb Ethernet feed. Some people pay to download every excruciating article
(like this one ;-) ) over a 2400 baud phone line.
If McElwaine had posted his "Peter Beter" letters once to one or two
newsgroups, we would have all had a good laugh, the letters would have
become cult favorites on an archive somewhere, and McElwaine would still
have his Net access. Instead, he posts them repeatedly (2 or 3 times so
far this year) to a whole BUNCH of groups, and apparently never responds
to e-mail. A successful formula for Net-ectomy.

>Just a personal observation. Feel free to enlighten me, if I have
>somehow misunderstood "house rules", I am still a new-comer to the
>Internet and would appreciate edification/education concerning this
>matter.

Never be ashamed to admit honest ignorance. We are ALL of us ignorant
about something. Anyone who flames you for admitting ignorance is a fool
and should be ignored. Now STUPIDITY, that's another story...

On the Net, you learn by watching and doing. Sometimes, old-timers get a
tad too smug and forget they were once newbies. If you're ever in doubt,
e-mail a friendly veteran. There are a few.


And as for McElwaine, here's my Official Psychic Prediction: he'll be
back!


