No.  You are.  You apparently do not appreciate how things work.  Try filing
a complaint alleging that your university has violated FERPA or perhaps CRA
1964 sometime, and watch them run and wag their tails.

Schools take these things *very* seriously.  If it rarely happens that
schools actually lose funding, it is because they hop to attention as
quickly as possible, in order to avoid losing funding.

A year or two ago, the Chronicle of Higher Education ran a piece from a
professor who had caught a student cheating, red handed, and subsequently
found evidence that the student had copied every test answer from another
student.  He failed the student.

The student's father, an attorney, asked to see the evidence of cheating.
Unfamiliar with the requirements of FERPA, the professor provided the father
with copies of the exams for both students--and failed to delete the name of
the other student.

The father subsequently threatened the school with a FERPA lawsuit, and the
administration pressured the professor to ignore the cheating, on the advice
of its counsel.

(The professor got back at the administration by giving the cheat an "A.")


In any case, this is but one example; however, it is quite reflective of
the manner in which universities often respond to complaints in which their
federal funding may be threatened.  Remember, the federal funding includes
virtually every grant that faculty at the school have received, as well as
all federal financial aid to students.  Schools typically cannot afford to
take any chances with these monies.

And in the RM case, it would seem to be that the mere *threat* of a lawsuit
(one that didn't even threaten their funding) was sufficient to make UW-EC
move very slowly in pursuing further action.

(Yes, I know RM actually did file and win a suit, earlier--but the accounts
here seem to indicate that fear of an another lawsuit has guided UW-EC's
behavior.)


