>Several people have brought up the issue of the U.S. Constitution and
>the right to free speech in this discussion of McElwaine's having been
>booted off the Net.  In my understanding, the Constitutional protection 
>of free speech is limited to situations in which the Federal Government
>is involved.  This is because, as I understand it (not being an
>attorney or a student of such matters) the Constitution says something
>like, "Congress shall make no law restricting [list of rights and
>freedoms], freedom of speech, ...."  This wording limits the scope of the
>"right to free speech" to situations regarding the law and to those
>involving federal money.  Privately funded colleges and universities
>that receive no government money are, for example, exempt.  
>
>I hope someone will correct this if I am mistaken.

You are, and I will.  (OK, sorry, I couldn't resist.)

Most (but not necessarily all) of the rights protected by the Bill of Rights
are "selectively incorporated" into the due process clause of the 14th
amendment.  The 14th amendment *does* apply to the states.

The rights to free speech and free press are among the rights that have been
ruled to be incorporated into the 14th amendment.  As such, they apply to
the states.

As far as state schools go, funding (from either the state, or the federal
government) is not the critical factor in determining whether the school
acts as an arm of the state.  The critical factor is whether the state has
some sort of legislative control over the institution.  (Such control could
be, for example, the ability to appoint the trustees, or the ability to
approve or disapprove rules enacted by the institution.)


