Message-ID: <126934@netnews.upenn.edu>
Date: 15 May 93 00:38:56 GMT

In an interview with the campus newspaper printed in the May 14th issue of
the newspaper, University of Pennsylvania President Sheldon Hackney
defended his record on free speech.

Hackney, who has come under attack for having allowed Penn freshman Eden
Jacobowitz to be brought up on charges of racial harassment, said in the
interview that "I am hardly a politically correct person of the left."

I have excerpted part of the interview:

Newspaper: One thing that is of some concern to critics, such as George
Will, is that you have sort of a selective defense of free expression.
When the threat comes from the right you're there defending free
expression.  But when [the threat to free speech] comes from the left,
you're less inclined to do so.  And they'll point to Mapplethorpe and
Serrano [both of whom Pres. Hackney defended] to show that you really
defended free speech when conservatives attacked these people.  But in
these cases, when attacks on free speech come from the other direction,
you are not so quick to defend free expression.  How do you answer that?

Hackney: Innocent. Innocent. I have defended free speech on campus left,
right and center.

Newspaper: Can you give an example of when you defended free speech when
the threat to it came from the left?

Hackney: In my experience, most threats to disrupt speakers on campus in
recent years have come from the left.

Newspaper: Could you cite an example?

Hackney: Well, I'm not very good at remembering exactly the names of
controversial individuals. Well, let's see who is the preacher? Jerry
Fallwell came and spoke here early in the '80s.  We provided the same
level of protection for him as for Louis Farrakahn.  This is something
that is really fundamental to me and actually I am offended when people
think I play ideological games with it.  I am hardly a politically correct
person of the left. I believe in free speech and open expression.


.......
earlier in the interview:

Newspaper: Eden Jacobowitz said he wrote to you shortly after [the
incident in January] occured, asking for your help. [Actually, according
to Jacobowitz's letter Rush Limbaugh read on radio, Jacobowitz contacted
Hackney on January 15th]

Hackney: I don't remember that. I don't remember that. Professor [Alan]
Kors, his advisor, certainly has communicated with me vigorously asking me
to step in.  But if you look at the procedures, you'll notice the
president and the provost don't appear there - theoretically until the
very end. We don't have a specific role - it is the vice provost for
University life who reports to the provost, who reports to me.  And I
would guess in important cases the vice provost would certainly consult
with us before doing anything in response to the panel's recommendations....




