From caf-talk Caf May 15 13:10:31 1993
Newsgroups: alt.comp.acad-freedom.talk
From: kadie@eff.org (Carl M. Kadie)
Subject: State schools enforcing charters
Message-ID: <1993May15.170959.15534@eff.org>
Date: Sat, 15 May 1993 17:09:59 GMT

The McElwaine situation shows why it might be nice to have Netnews
sites enforce newsgroup charters. What would a Netnews facility at a
state school have to do to enforce charters?

1. It can't enforce all charters. It might be able to enforce topic
and manner (e.g. length of article) restrictions, but it could not
enforce viewpoint restrictions, tone restrictions, or any
discrimination that violates school policy. For example, if it allows
pro-gay articles to be posted in soc.motss, it could not prohibit
anti-gay articles in soc.motss. If an unmoderated *.women-only
newsgroup came back, the school probably couldn't stop men from
posting to it. Likewise it couldn't prohibit flames, offensive posts,
etc.

2. The restrictions would have to be explicit. This would mean a
written policy that off-topic articles, for example, are prohibited.
Also, the topics would have to be defined. This might be accomplished
by having a copy of the charters available easily (probably locally).

3. The restrictions could not be enforced selectively. If a site lets
everyone post off-topic except that one person who generates
complaints because of his viewpoint, then the school is really
discrimination on the basis of complaints/viewpoint.

4. The restrictions should be enforced with due process.

- Carl

