As far as constitutionality goes, it would appear to be quite simple.

These are time, place and manner restrictions.  Under law (Clark v. Community
for Creative Nonviolence (1984)), time, place and manner restrictions must
be "narrowly drawn to further a substantial government interest...unrelated
to the suppression of free speech."

The kind of restrictions you describe are certainly not narrowly drawn, and
may well have been formulated for the purpose of discouraging or suppressing
expression.


